## 7. SUPABASE DATABASE SCHEMA

Create SQL migration files in a supabase/migrations directory.

The application must provide a documented, repeatable database setup process.

Create the following tables or an equivalent normalized schema.

### A. profiles

Fields:
- id UUID PRIMARY KEY REFERENCES auth.users(id)
- display_name TEXT
- job_title TEXT
- work_unit TEXT
- created_at TIMESTAMPTZ
- updated_at TIMESTAMPTZ

Use the authenticated user's UUID as the profile ID.

### B. shift_types

Fields:
- id UUID PRIMARY KEY
- code TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL
- name TEXT NOT NULL
- color_hex TEXT NOT NULL
- description TEXT
- sort_order INTEGER
- is_active BOOLEAN DEFAULT TRUE
- created_at TIMESTAMPTZ

Seed exactly six records:
KUNING, BIRU, UNGU, ABU_ABU, HIJAU, PINK.

Seed the colors and descriptions defined in this prompt.

Reference shift profiles must not be duplicated every time a user signs up.

### C. shift_tasks

Fields:
- id UUID PRIMARY KEY
- shift_type_id UUID REFERENCES shift_types(id)
- task_name TEXT NOT NULL
- sort_order INTEGER
- created_at TIMESTAMPTZ

Seed the supplied task lists for the six shift types.

### D. coworkers

Fields:
- id UUID PRIMARY KEY
- user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES auth.users(id)
- name TEXT NOT NULL
- nickname TEXT
- notes TEXT
- is_active BOOLEAN DEFAULT TRUE
- created_at TIMESTAMPTZ
- updated_at TIMESTAMPTZ

These are personal contact records, not hospital accounts.

### E. shifts

Fields:
- id UUID PRIMARY KEY
- user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES auth.users(id)
- shift_date DATE NOT NULL
- shift_type_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES shift_types(id)
- start_time TIME
- end_time TIME
- location TEXT
- notes TEXT
- status TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'scheduled'
- original_shift_type_id UUID REFERENCES shift_types(id)
- is_confirmed BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE
- created_at TIMESTAMPTZ
- updated_at TIMESTAMPTZ

Use valid status values such as:
- scheduled
- changed
- cancelled

Validate status values using a PostgreSQL CHECK constraint or enum.

Do not assume that all shifts have the same hours.

Allow shifts that cross midnight. Store the date as the shift start date and explicitly handle overnight shifts in the UI and conflict detection.

The application must support multiple shifts on one date so the user can record and investigate possible conflicts.

Do not add a UNIQUE constraint on user_id and shift_date because it would prevent the application from identifying duplicate or overlapping assignments.

### F. shift_coworkers

Fields:
- id UUID PRIMARY KEY
- shift_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES shifts(id) ON DELETE CASCADE
- coworker_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES coworkers(id) ON DELETE CASCADE
- created_at TIMESTAMPTZ
- UNIQUE (shift_id, coworker_id)

This table connects coworkers to an individual shift.

### G. shift_task_checks

Fields:
- id UUID PRIMARY KEY
- user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES auth.users(id)
- shift_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES shifts(id) ON DELETE CASCADE
- task_name TEXT NOT NULL
- is_completed BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE
- completed_at TIMESTAMPTZ
- created_at TIMESTAMPTZ
- updated_at TIMESTAMPTZ

Each shift has its own checklist state.

Do not automatically reset or overwrite historical task checklists when the next shift is created.

### H. shift_change_logs

Fields:
- id UUID PRIMARY KEY
- user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES auth.users(id)
- shift_id UUID REFERENCES shifts(id) ON DELETE SET NULL
- change_type TEXT NOT NULL
- old_shift_date DATE
- new_shift_date DATE
- old_shift_type_id UUID REFERENCES shift_types(id)
- new_shift_type_id UUID REFERENCES shift_types(id)
- old_start_time TIME
- new_start_time TIME
- old_end_time TIME
- new_end_time TIME
- reason TEXT
- source TEXT
- source_message TEXT
- created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW()

Possible sources:
- manual
- chat_text
- screenshot
- imported

The change log is append-only for normal application users. Users must not silently overwrite historical change records.

When a shift is edited or cancelled, save the original and new values in a change log.

Do not write a change log for an edit that made no actual changes.

### I. personal_notes

Fields:
- id UUID PRIMARY KEY
- user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES auth.users(id)
- shift_id UUID REFERENCES shifts(id) ON DELETE SET NULL
- note_date DATE
- title TEXT NOT NULL
- content TEXT
- is_pinned BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE
- created_at TIMESTAMPTZ
- updated_at TIMESTAMPTZ

### J. reminder_settings

Fields:
- id UUID PRIMARY KEY
- user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES auth.users(id)
- enabled BOOLEAN DEFAULT TRUE
- day_before_enabled BOOLEAN DEFAULT TRUE
- day_before_time TIME DEFAULT '20:00'
- minutes_before_shift INTEGER DEFAULT 60
- browser_notifications_enabled BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE
- created_at TIMESTAMPTZ
- updated_at TIMESTAMPTZ

Add any additional table or index required for correctness, but avoid unnecessary complexity.

Create indexes for common lookups:
- shifts(user_id, shift_date)
- coworkers(user_id, name)
- shift_change_logs(user_id, created_at DESC)
- personal_notes(user_id, note_date)

## 8. ROW LEVEL SECURITY

Enable RLS on all user-owned tables.

Create secure policies that ensure:
- profiles.id = auth.uid()
- coworkers.user_id = auth.uid()
- shifts.user_id = auth.uid()
- personal_notes.user_id = auth.uid()
- shift_change_logs.user_id = auth.uid()
- reminder_settings.user_id = auth.uid()
- shift_task_checks.user_id = auth.uid()

For shift_coworkers, validate ownership through the parent shift and the coworker.

Do not rely only on frontend filters for security.

Prevent users from attaching another user's coworker record to their shift.

Protect shared shift_types and shift_tasks reference data from arbitrary modification by ordinary authenticated users. Seed these records through SQL migrations.

For shift logs, implement append-only behavior as far as practical, using restrictive RLS and/or database triggers.

Use appropriate foreign keys, constraints, and indexes.

Document the policies in the SQL migrations.

